From 370a9377b05f881904133e4130bb0b2afa3ec3a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:47:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 37/44] pulse: report no delay while audio is still held back

Audio waiting for the clock to settle is not queued, so the stream knows
nothing about it, and what the stream does report is the silence it is
running on meanwhile. Answering with that describes neither.

aout_DecSynchronize() runs before the buffer it is about to hand over is
queued, so at the moment the clock settles it read a delay of 135 ms -
the priming silence - against a pts a second out, concluded the audio was
a second early, and padded a second of silence to meet it. The pad then
made the same second late, and it was jumped over:

  playback way too early (-1014190): playing silence
  playback way too late  (1013202): jumping ahead

A second of silence, and a second of material dropped to pay for it, at
roughly two starts in three.

Say nothing until the audio is actually in. Captured from the sink's
monitor over fourteen seconds, silence falls from 7-8% to 0.4-0.7%, and
what is left is 25-50 ms at each repeat. Rate is unchanged: three repeats
of a four second file per fifteen second run, 14.2-15.4 s of audio
reaching the device.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 modules/audio_output/pulse.c | 10 ++++++----
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/modules/audio_output/pulse.c b/modules/audio_output/pulse.c
index 3a77d19775..9e11099f30 100644
--- a/modules/audio_output/pulse.c
+++ b/modules/audio_output/pulse.c
@@ -651,10 +651,12 @@ static int TimeGet(audio_output_t *aout, vlc_tick_t *restrict delay)
     int ret = -1;
 
     pa_threaded_mainloop_lock(sys->mainloop);
-    /* While the stream is running on silence to measure the device latency,
-     * what it reports describes the silence, not the audio. Reporting it
-     * would have the core treat the padding as lateness and flush. */
-    if (stream_clock_is_synced(s))
+    /* Anything held back is not queued yet, so the stream does not account
+     * for it. Answering with the silence it is running on instead describes
+     * neither: the core reads audio that is about to be let go all at once as
+     * a second of earliness, pads a second of silence to meet it, then finds
+     * that same second late and jumps over it. Say nothing until it is in. */
+    if (sys->fifo.first == NULL && stream_clock_is_synced(s))
     {
         vlc_tick_t delta = vlc_pa_get_latency(aout, sys->context, s);
         if (delta != VLC_TICK_INVALID)
